            <ArticleTitle>Late, but not early, arriving younger siblings foster firstborns' understanding of second-order false belief.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>251-265</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0022-0965(16)30191-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jecp.2017.08.007</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This study examined the influence of younger siblings on children's understanding of second-order false belief. In a representative community sample of firstborn children (N=229) with a mean age of 7years (SD=4.58), false belief was assessed during a home visit using an adaptation of a well-established second-order false belief narrative enacted with Playmobil figures. Children's responses were coded to establish performance on second-order false belief questions. When controlling for verbal IQ and age, the existence of a younger sibling predicted a twofold advantage in children's second-order false belief performance, yet this was the case only for firstborns who experienced the arrival of a sibling after their second birthday. These findings provide a foundation for future research on family influences on social cognition.</AbstractText>
